<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">White Sea–Baltic Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as White Sea Canal, Belomorkanal, Stalin White Sea–Baltic Canal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a>–Baltic Canal is a ship canal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, opened on 2 August 1933, that runs 227 kilometres from the White Sea to Lake Onega and so to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. It was the first major Soviet project built with forced labour: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> inmates dug it in twenty months between 1931 and 1933, almost entirely by hand, and between 12,000 and 25,000 of them died according to official records. Presented as a showcase of the first five-year plan and of convicts 'reforging' themselves through labour, it was praised in a book compiled by a brigade of 120 writers and artists organised by Maxim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Gorky</a>. Yet at 3.5 metres deep it was too shallow for most seagoing ships.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-sea-baltic-canal.jpg" alt="White Sea–Baltic Canal" width="330" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexxx1979 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Sea–Baltic_Canal,_lock_P7120376_2200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
